# QA environment secrets. Copy to `.env` next to docker-compose.qa.yml on the
# QA droplet (chmod 600) and fill in. NEVER commit the real .env.

# Postgres (QA-local container; data survives in the qa_pgdata volume)
DB_USERNAME=townbasket
DB_PASSWORD=change-me-qa-db-password

# JWT signing key — random, >=32 bytes. Different from production's.
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-32-byte-minimum-secret

# Basic-auth gate for the three app hosts (NOT the api).
# Generate: docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext 'your-qa-password'
# KEEP THE SINGLE QUOTES: unquoted, Compose's env-file interpolation expands the
# hash's $-sequences (e.g. "$ruHz") as variables and silently corrupts it.
QA_BASIC_AUTH_HASH='change-me-bcrypt-hash'

# Storefront Google Maps browser key (optional in QA; pin-drop is disabled
# gracefully when empty). If set, add the qa domain to the key's referrer list.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# ---- Real mobile-OTP verification in QA (optional) -------------------------
#
# Left as-is, QA runs the offline fake verifier: any 10-digit phone, OTP token
# dev:<phone>, no SMS, no cost. Uncomment ALL SEVEN lines below to exercise the
# real Firebase phone-OTP path instead. Do it as one change — a storefront that
# requests a real SMS against a backend still on the fake (or the reverse) fails
# every login.
#
# The first line is a PASS-THROUGH in docker-compose.qa.yml: it must be either a
# real projectId or absent. Do NOT leave it present-but-blank — blank is never
# read as "use the fake" (that would downgrade a real deployment to a verifier
# accepting any phone number), so the API refuses to boot and names the property.
#
# PREFER A SEPARATE FIREBASE PROJECT for QA (e.g. town-basket-qa). Auth users and
# test phone numbers live per Firebase project, so reusing production's mixes QA
# logins into the production user pool. Reusing it works — the values are in
# infra/deploy/.env.example — but it is not clean.
#
# In the Firebase console first:
#   1. Authentication -> Sign-in method -> enable Phone.
#   2. Authentication -> Settings -> Authorized domains -> add qa.town-basket.com.
#   3. Phone -> "Phone numbers for testing": add each QA tester number with a
#      fixed 6-digit code. Those numbers verify WITHOUT sending an SMS, so QA
#      stays free and deterministic while running the real code path. Strongly
#      preferred over billing real SMS to test logins.
#
# Backend (activates the real Google-signed-token verifier). Copy the projectId
# VERBATIM from the console — Firebase appends a generated suffix to a taken
# name, so "town-basket-qa" may really be "town-basket-qa-baf89", and the
# verifier matches iss/aud exactly. It must equal NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
# below; a mismatch 401s every login with "Invalid phone token".
# TOWNBASKET_IDENTITY_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=town-basket-qa-xxxxx
#
# Storefront BUILD args — Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* at build time, so after
# changing these run: docker compose -f docker-compose.qa.yml up -d --build storefront
# Paste these straight from Firebase console -> Project settings -> General ->
# Your apps -> Web app (the firebaseConfig object). measurementId is not used —
# the storefront deliberately does not load Analytics.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=town-basket-qa-xxxxx.firebaseapp.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=town-basket-qa-xxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=town-basket-qa-xxxxx.firebasestorage.app
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=


# ---- Log shipping to Grafana Cloud (optional) ------------------------------
# A small Alloy agent ships EVERY container's logs (api, storefront, admin,
# delivery, caddy, postgres) to Grafana Cloud Loki, where you view and search
# them in a browser — and they survive the droplet. OFF by default.
#
# To turn it on, set the profile line AND all three GRAFANA_LOKI_* values, then
# redeploy. Every future `docker compose up -d` starts the agent automatically.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=monitoring
#
# From a free Grafana Cloud account -> Connections -> add "Hosted logs" (Loki):
#   - URL   : the push endpoint, e.g. https://logs-prod-012.grafana.net/loki/api/v1/push
#   - USER  : the numeric instance / user id it shows as the username
#   - TOKEN : an access token / API key scoped logs:write (this is the password)
# The token is a SECRET — it belongs here in .env, never in git.
# GRAFANA_LOKI_URL=
# GRAFANA_LOKI_USER=
# GRAFANA_LOKI_TOKEN=


# ---- Web Push order notifications (optional) -------------------------------
# Browser/phone notifications for order updates, for customers and riders who
# opt in. OFF by default: with no keys the API logs "Web Push channel disabled"
# on startup, the apps hide the opt-in button, and everything else behaves the
# same (in-app SSE still updates any open page).
#
# Generate the VAPID pair ONCE, on your own machine:
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# Reuse the same pair for the life of the environment — regenerating
# invalidates every existing subscription and everyone must opt in again. Use a
# DIFFERENT pair from production's, so a QA push can never reach a real
# customer's phone.
#
# WEB_PUSH_PRIVATE_KEY is a SECRET — it belongs here, never in git. The public
# key is handed to browsers and is not secret. WEB_PUSH_SUBJECT is the contact
# a push service uses to reach the operator about this sender; it must be a
# real mailto: or https: you monitor, and defaults to
# mailto:support@town-basket.com when left unset.
#
# After setting these, recreate the API container so it re-reads .env —
# `docker compose restart` does NOT pick up changed environment variables:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.qa.yml up -d api
# Then confirm: docker compose -f docker-compose.qa.yml logs api | grep "Web Push"
# WEB_PUSH_PUBLIC_KEY=
# WEB_PUSH_PRIVATE_KEY=
# WEB_PUSH_SUBJECT=mailto:support@town-basket.com

# ---- Product image uploads (optional) --------------------------------------
# Staff upload product photos in the admin catalogue; they are stored in a DO
# Space and served from its public URL. OFF by default: the API logs "Product
# image upload disabled" and the form falls back to pasting an image URL.
#
# Use a DIFFERENT bucket from production's — QA test photos must never be
# written into the bucket the real shop serves from — and one separate from any
# backup bucket, since these objects are world-readable.
#
# CATALOG_IMAGES_SECRET_KEY is a SECRET — it belongs here, never in git.
# CATALOG_IMAGES_BUCKET=town-basket-media-qa
# CATALOG_IMAGES_ENDPOINT=https://blr1.digitaloceanspaces.com
# CATALOG_IMAGES_REGION=blr1
# CATALOG_IMAGES_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://town-basket-media-qa.blr1.digitaloceanspaces.com
# CATALOG_IMAGES_ACCESS_KEY=
# CATALOG_IMAGES_SECRET_KEY=
